WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.195
In Hello, Lattice, you registered Lattice
on a silo and wrote and read typed values.

2
00:00:08.545 --> 00:00:13.548
Now choose what should happen when two
writers change the same key concurrently.

3
00:00:14.548 --> 00:00:16.509
A plain set keeps the last write.

4
00:00:16.509 --> 00:00:19.362
It does not combine
the contents of two records.

5
00:00:19.362 --> 00:00:22.691
If both updates matter,
replacing one is the wrong rule.

6
00:00:23.041 --> 00:00:26.769
A conflict-free replicated data type makes
that rule part of the value.

7
00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:30.603
Choose the rule that matches your data,
not just the shape of its fields.

8
00:00:31.603 --> 00:00:35.467
For a total that only grows,
choose a grow-only counter.

9
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:38.158
Each writer advances its own component.

10
00:00:38.508 --> 00:00:42.301
Here, cluster A adds three,
while cluster B adds five.

11
00:00:42.301 --> 00:00:44.830
Neither has seen the other's change.

12
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.297
Merge takes the larger component
for each writer.

13
00:00:48.297 --> 00:00:52.051
Both components survive,
and the value is their sum: eight.

14
00:00:52.401 --> 00:00:55.560
Deliver the same state again
and the value stays eight.

15
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.637
For a count that can also decrease,
choose a positive-negative counter:

16
00:00:59.637 --> 00:01:02.279
separate totals
for increments and decrements.

17
00:01:03.279 --> 00:01:05.766
For a collection that only gains members,

18
00:01:05.766 --> 00:01:08.981
a grow-only set combines the members
from both sides.

19
00:01:09.331 --> 00:01:15.005
If members can be removed, choose what
a concurrent add and remove should mean.

20
00:01:15.005 --> 00:01:17.663
An observed-remove set keeps the add.

21
00:01:18.013 --> 00:01:20.648
A remove-wins set makes
the opposite choice.

22
00:01:20.648 --> 00:01:23.164
That choice is part of the data's meaning,

23
00:01:23.164 --> 00:01:26.039
not a setting to leave
until a conflict happens.

24
00:01:27.039 --> 00:01:29.642
Sometimes neither write should disappear.

25
00:01:29.642 --> 00:01:33.261
A multi-value register keeps
values written concurrently.

26
00:01:33.611 --> 00:01:35.154
Both clusters started from todo.

27
00:01:35.154 --> 00:01:37.759
One writes in progress,
and the other writes resolved,

28
00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:39.350
without seeing the other's write.

29
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.373
The merged register holds both.

30
00:01:41.373 --> 00:01:45.151
Your application reads the candidates
and decides how to resolve them.

31
00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:49.631
A later write, made after observing
both values, replaces both.

32
00:01:49.631 --> 00:01:55.137
Two writes in a row on one cluster are not
this conflict: the second sees the first.

33
00:01:56.137 --> 00:02:00.028
For a value that should only move upwards,
choose a maximum register;

34
00:02:00.028 --> 00:02:03.074
for one that only moves downwards,
a minimum register.

35
00:02:03.424 --> 00:02:08.459
For a dictionary whose values also need
to merge, choose an observed-remove map.

36
00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:13.180
Its keys are add-wins, and each value
merges by its own conflict-free rule.

37
00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:18.706
The typed accessor chooses the merge mode
and encodes the delta.

38
00:02:18.706 --> 00:02:21.393
You do not hand-encode the CRDT delta.

39
00:02:21.393 --> 00:02:25.141
Give independent writers stable,
distinct identities.

40
00:02:25.491 --> 00:02:28.638
A local tree can mix these types
on different keys.

41
00:02:28.638 --> 00:02:32.156
A replicated tree must use
the single merge mode declared

42
00:02:32.156 --> 00:02:33.575
in its replication map.

43
00:02:33.925 --> 00:02:38.749
Maps also need their key and value shape
registered on the host, once per tree.

44
00:02:38.749 --> 00:02:43.206
The CRDT guide has the accessor examples
and the details for each choice.

45
00:02:44.206 --> 00:02:46.701
Next on Build: Scans, filters and cursors.

46
00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:50.858
The deep dive on how this works is
Conflict-free merges in 90 seconds.

47
00:02:50.858 --> 00:02:54.303
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
